WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.720
Red Hat OpenShift is the enterprise
Kubernetes platform,

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.040
and with the latest version, OpenShift 4,

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.080
the platform has undergone significant improvements to developer experience,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.200
automation, and the management of the platform itself.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.020
But what exactly changed, and what do you need to know about OpenShift 4?

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.560
Let's get started.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.440
So, OpenShift is Kubernetes at the core,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.700
and with OpenShift 4, the platform is now driven by Operators.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.980
Yes, this includes the services that support OpenShift,

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.360
as well as app services that are deployed by users like you.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.800
This is significant.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.220
So, we'll start with covering Operators.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.660
Next, we'll jump into one of the first things users will notice in OpenShift 4

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.040
- that is, an improved developer experience.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.300
And this comes with significant updates to the console.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.840
And then finally we'll dive into some of the community-driven projects

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.240
that OpenShift has adopted into supported solutions.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.760
Things like OpenShift Service Mesh and OpenShift Pipelines.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.880
There's actually more but we'll touch on these two today.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:05.660
Starting with Operators, essentially they
allow you to automate the lifecycle of

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.909
containers. Let's say I'm deploying a
simple front-end and back-end

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:14.130
application. Once I deploy all of them
into my cluster, I've got to manage all

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.960
the automation or on the config of
individual applications, but with an

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.850
Operator I can take a different approach.
By installing an Operator into a cluster

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:30.659
with OLM, or Operator Lifecycle Manager, I can enable new CRDs, or Custom Resource

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:35.460
Definitions. These CRDs allow me to
manage my application using custom

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.930
config files tailored from my
application. In addition, any automation

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.040
that I need can be built into the
operator itself. Essentially I've

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.409
extended the Kubernetes API to create
new custom resources that are tailored

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:55.290
to the resources that you regularly work
with. In OpenShift 4, the services that

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:59.310
make up OpenShift itself are actually
managed by Operators. This means we get

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.990
to take advantage of that same framework to easily do installation and upgrades

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.890
of OpenShift itself. Check out the
Operator SDK to create your own

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.930
Operators, or use the embedded Operator
Hub and OpenShift to start quickly

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.920
and with
existing solutions. Next let's talk about

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.310
one of the first things you'll notice in
the platform and that is an improved

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.430
developer experience starting from the
console.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.599
The main thing you'll notice is a
different view for administrators and

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:32.519
developers. There's new dashboard
capabilities for streamlined deployment

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:37.590
of applications, whether you start with a
git repo, container image, or deployment

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:42.629
YAML. In addition, you'll get better
observability into the platform. For

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:46.109
example, there's an advanced view which
tracks everything happening in your

00:02:46.109 --> 00:02:50.519
cluster. There's improved administration
of the cluster itself, with a new user

00:02:50.519 --> 00:02:55.560
manage section as well. Lastly, let's
close with some of the community driven

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.390
projects that OpenShift is supporting. One
of them is going to be OpenShift

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:05.700
Pipelines with Tekton. Tekon is a
cloud native way to declare CI/CD

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.739
pipelines and it's based entirely on
Kubernetes. It starts with defining the

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:15.870
tasks that make up a CI/CD flow, which
actually run as pods in your cluster.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:21.030
These make up a pipeline and that's able
to deploy applications into your cluster.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:25.530
In OpenShift there's actually UI
integration between Tekton and OpenShift.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:31.760
So, OpenShift Pipelines lets you
manage your CI/CD all in the dashboard.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.000
The other community-driven project I
want to talk about is OpenShift

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.010
Service Mesh. This is based on Istio.
Imagine you have a number of services

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.760
that are dependent on one another, a
number of concerns arise in the

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.440
interaction between these services. How
do you actually manage these

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:53.760
interdependent complexities? Well instead
of managing them in the app itself, you

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:59.099
can take advantage of the Istio Control
Plane which uses "sidecars" to basically

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:02.549
help you control how these microservices connect with each other, how

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:07.620
they enforce policies, and then even
observe how they behave. That way the

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:13.530
capabilities rest on the control plane
rather than the apps themselves. Thanks

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:17.099
for joining me for this quick video overviewing some of the newest features in
OpenShift 4.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.740
As always, if you like the
video or you have any comments, please

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.700
drop a like or a comment below. Be sure
to subscribe and stay tuned for more

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.880
videos like this in the future.

